#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266143 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119870 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "37209e5f-a6e8-55d5-b76f-19f79db67566" :knowledge-point/title "Computing 4x4 Determinants Using Row and Column Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12d46ac6-9747-5c71-b490-546887e78256" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "256" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263178} {:answer/id #uuid "2afea5e5-8fc7-5005-883c-6aaf5bb2ea37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "324" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263367} {:answer/id #uuid "65602076-c5b7-5aad-8a7a-cd4f344a6872" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "315" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263878} {:answer/id #uuid "844ee3e5-405e-5c43-8fad-90753e6eb5f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "312" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264134} {:answer/id #uuid "efbf8c8d-59c7-55fc-afcd-b1821ae25cb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "278" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265103}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2afea5e5-8fc7-5005-883c-6aaf5bb2ea37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "324" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263367} :answer-field/id #uuid "653fc202-e598-5222-9f0e-b11e0cf8ccc7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265422}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1e03835d-437b-59ee-bd2c-565e5373dfa5" :question/math-academy-id "q-132144" :question/problem "Calculate the value of \n$\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 7 & -5 \\\\ -2 & 3 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 1 & 7\\end{aligned}|.$\n\n*Hint: Use elementary row operations to make the entries above and below ${a}_{32}$ equal to zero. You should be able to reduce the given $4\\times 4$ determinant to a $3\\times 3$ determinant.*" :question/worked-solution "Using elementary row operations, let's make entries above and below ${a}_{32}=3$ equal to zero.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 7 & -5 \\\\ -2 & 3 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 1 & 7\\end{aligned}| &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{3} \\\\ {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{3} \\\\ {R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+(-1){R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & -4 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 3 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 5\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $2$nd column, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & -4 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 3 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-1)^{3+2}|\\begin{aligned}-3 & -4 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 6 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-3|\\begin{aligned}-3 & -4 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 6 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot -3\\cdot (-1)^{1+1}|\\begin{aligned}6 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =9(30+6) \\\\  & =324.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484254 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119874 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b0edd60-2801-59d3-b9df-18bfbf8763bb" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a 4x4 Determinant to a 3x3 Determinant Using Row Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d711e45-7ec3-56d2-ab35-037569a4df11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-|\\begin{aligned}-3 & -5 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 2 & -4\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483744} {:answer/id #uuid "7e8a1859-b3ba-56f4-b537-6223471bd407" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-|\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -6 \\\\ 4 & 7 & -8\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483825} {:answer/id #uuid "b8679840-e1d7-55b1-9b60-168dd43e13d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-|\\begin{aligned}-3 & -5 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 2 & -4 \\\\ -4 & -8 & 8\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483937} {:answer/id #uuid "db442710-d557-5f6a-8536-d434b1d8713a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-|\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -6 & -8 & 7\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484004} {:answer/id #uuid "f05253ae-92cc-554f-a9fa-70f4587815bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 & 8 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 7 & -8 & 6\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484044}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db442710-d557-5f6a-8536-d434b1d8713a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-|\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -6 & -8 & 7\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484004} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e61c18c-5fab-5f5b-87cc-ad4a3cb34dce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484155}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "988956df-fafe-5af5-9e6f-367acfc61887" :question/math-academy-id "q-42570" :question/problem "$$\n|\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & -5 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 2 & -4 \\\\ -1 & -4 & -8 & 8\\end{aligned}|\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3\\times 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary row operations to make the entries above and below ${a}_{21}$ equal to zero.*" :question/worked-solution "Let's make entries above and below ${a}_{21}=1$ equal to zero using elementary row operations.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & -5 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 2 & -4 \\\\ -1 & -4 & -8 & 8\\end{aligned}| &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -6 & -8 & 7\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $1$st column, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -6 & -8 & 7\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{2+1}|\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -6 & -8 & 7\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-|\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -6 & -8 & 7\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484204 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119870 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "37209e5f-a6e8-55d5-b76f-19f79db67566" :knowledge-point/title "Computing 4x4 Determinants Using Row and Column Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06c6938e-502c-5f98-9c7a-0ebfcfb54e7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-68" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483582} {:answer/id #uuid "4f852e05-57a9-502f-b965-9fd1fbb17ae7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-66" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483713} {:answer/id #uuid "a098acd6-da89-5cfe-bde4-ac323858939a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-110" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483898} {:answer/id #uuid "c4678384-56ad-53be-a089-e4e17f325669" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "77" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483955} {:answer/id #uuid "f23d61e3-4fd0-5603-9dfe-41cc76943e7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484052}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4f852e05-57a9-502f-b965-9fd1fbb17ae7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-66" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483713} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9aaf06e-8aeb-522a-88ab-797652cf646e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484185}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "20f4c241-f6b2-5f00-9ec7-bcba6743c7a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-42919" :question/problem "Calculate the value of \n$\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -6 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 4 & 3 \\\\ -5 & -1 & 10 & 1\\end{aligned}|.$\n\n*Hint: Use elementary row operations to make the entries below ${a}_{13}$ equal to zero. You should be able to reduce the given $4\\times 4$ determinant to a $3\\times 3$ determinant.*" :question/worked-solution "Using elementary row operations, let's make entries below ${a}_{13}=-2$ equal to zero.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -6 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 4 & 3 \\\\ -5 & -1 & 10 & 1\\end{aligned}| &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+5{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 10 & 0 & -7 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 0 & -11 & 0 & 11\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $3$rd column, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 10 & 0 & -7 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 0 & -11 & 0 & 11\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot (-1)^{1+3}|\\begin{aligned}0 & 10 & -7 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 7 \\\\ 0 & -11 & 11\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2|\\begin{aligned}0 & 10 & -7 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 7 \\\\ 0 & -11 & 11\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot (-1)\\cdot (-1)^{2+1}|\\begin{aligned}10 & -7 \\\\ -11 & 11\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2(110-77) \\\\  & =-66.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120545 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119875 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "01ff3b37-83e6-506a-a6e8-07858a2bcb66" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a 4x4 Determinant to a 3x3 Determinant Using Column Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fec93138-cf0b-5d3f-8a5c-8882046ae062" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -5 & 4 \\\\ -10 & -5 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 3 & -4\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394835} {:answer/id #uuid "090d23f3-9df7-5a18-ad1c-3041ca3b0b88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2|\\begin{aligned}1 & -7 & 10 \\\\ -9 & -6 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 4 & -7\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394836} {:answer/id #uuid "c4475098-4c19-544f-a8c8-75b11b40afce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -5 & 4 \\\\ -10 & -4 & 6 \\\\ 5 & 3 & -4\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394837} {:answer/id #uuid "0720eaf4-1dba-52f4-98df-0fc8cc7f0d92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 10 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -6 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -7\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394838} {:answer/id #uuid "c47f2fc0-2674-5e12-89b3-daeb57faaf22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2|\\begin{aligned}-3 & -6 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -6 \\\\ 1 & 4 & -7\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394839}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fec93138-cf0b-5d3f-8a5c-8882046ae062" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -5 & 4 \\\\ -10 & -5 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 3 & -4\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394835} :answer-field/id #uuid "294d6d11-4a04-5aed-b0e6-84671260549e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394834}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2ea53fce-52af-53d1-b17c-6cd66b5ff019" :question/math-academy-id "q-91092" :question/problem "$$\n|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -7 & 10 \\\\ -9 & -1 & -6 & 9 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 2 & -6 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 4 & -7\\end{aligned}|\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3\\times 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary column operations to make the entries to the left and to the right of ${a}_{32}$ equal to zero.*" :question/worked-solution "Let's make entries to the left and to the right of ${a}_{32}=2$ equal to zero using elementary column operations.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -7 & 10 \\\\ -9 & -1 & -6 & 9 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 2 & -6 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 4 & -7\\end{aligned}| &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:={C}_{1}+{C}_{2} \\\\ {C}_{3}:={C}_{3}+(-1){C}_{2} \\\\ {C}_{4}:={C}_{4}+3{C}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & -5 & 4 \\\\ -10 & -1 & -5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 3 & -4\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $3$rd row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & -5 & 4 \\\\ -10 & -1 & -5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 3 & -4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)^{3+2}|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -5 & 4 \\\\ -10 & -5 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 3 & -4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -5 & 4 \\\\ -10 & -5 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 3 & -4\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484245 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119875 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "01ff3b37-83e6-506a-a6e8-07858a2bcb66" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a 4x4 Determinant to a 3x3 Determinant Using Column Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17d6e884-c166-55a3-a97c-9e435db64b06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -2\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483607} {:answer/id #uuid "5e4476d2-7b18-522a-b167-52cbab3f3c3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3|\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -2\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483748} {:answer/id #uuid "8b44067d-79fe-52e7-82bd-1255f8daff64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -1 & -2\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483854} {:answer/id #uuid "9e5f3ec9-451c-5e08-9424-a012a9cef0b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3|\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 8 & 6 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & -1\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483895} {:answer/id #uuid "d5ed131f-b891-52a5-9551-160b86e31eff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3|\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 8 & 6 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 9 & 7\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483994}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e5f3ec9-451c-5e08-9424-a012a9cef0b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3|\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 8 & 6 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & -1\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483895} :answer-field/id #uuid "773fbcca-312d-53ef-8e00-a56e31c49e76" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484139}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "858b58f0-f246-57e1-b5d7-2b4a24d86dda" :question/math-academy-id "q-91200" :question/problem "$$\n|\\begin{aligned}9 & 3 & 6 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -1 & -2\\end{aligned}|\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3\\times 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary column operations to make the entries to the left and to the right of ${a}_{12}$ equal to zero.*" :question/worked-solution "Let's make entries to the left and to the right of ${a}_{12}=3$ equal to zero using elementary column operations.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}9 & 3 & 6 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -1 & -2\\end{aligned}| &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:={C}_{1}+(-3){C}_{2} \\\\ {C}_{3}:={C}_{3}+(-2){C}_{2} \\\\ {C}_{4}:={C}_{4}+{C}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 8 & -2 & 6 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -3 & -1\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $1$st row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 8 & -2 & 6 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -3 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-1)^{1+2}|\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 8 & 6 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-3|\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 8 & 6 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & -1\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
